{
  "id": "companions",
  "title": "Service classes",
  "summary": "A service is a class that holds the state and behavior of a view. A view refers to a service by its instance name: `BasketService` is `basket`. `{basket.count}` reads a field, and `on:click=\"basket.add()\"` calls a method. Fields hold the state of the view. Methods give its behavior. By default, a service is a page singleton (one shared instance for the whole page). Every component that names it shares that one instance. `NeedsAll` creates one service instance for each component instance. It uses the properties of that component. `Lifetime({\"value\":\"prototype\"})` also gives other services one instance for each component. `OnStart` runs after injection (the framework gives the service the objects it needs). It also waits until the component is attached to the DOM (the tree of elements on the page). `OnStop` cleans up work that the service owns. A declared `applicationEventPublisher` field and `OnEvent` replace local publish and subscribe code that you would write by hand. A `Needs` field named after a browser resource receives that resource. There is no general framework object on the instance. Ordinary imports and helper functions still work. Constants are static class fields. A Python service writes `@on_start` above a plain or `async def` method and `@on_stop` and `@on_event` above plain methods, and its `application_event_publisher: ApplicationEventPublisher` field is the publisher. A C++ service writes `[[OnStart]]` and `[[OnEvent]]`, and its `ApplicationEventPublisher applicationEventPublisher;` field is the publisher.",
  "group": "Tac views",
